%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level5_MacLab
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_5
  levelNumber: 5
  displayName: The Compromised Campus Network
  sceneName: Room5_MacLab
  description: Suspicious messages have been reported in the university MacLab. Investigate
    the flagged workstations and decide what's real.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ec2bc026c46543c4788ea2715af04007, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 5: THE COMPROMISED CAMPUS NETWORK'
  briefingText: "A series of suspicious emails and messages has been reported in
    MacLab 3 at UPHSL Bi\xF1an. Five workstations have been flagged. Go to each one,
    investigate the message on screen, and decide whether it is a scam or legitimate.
    The instructor's computer holds the university's official contacts and policies
    - compare before you decide."
  caseNotes:
  - "You are a UPHSL Bi\xF1an student helping the campus security team in MacLab
    3."
  - Your campus account is active - you passed the account check last month.
  - You did not apply for any scholarship or grant this term.
  - Your tuition for this term is fully paid (receipt on file).
  objectives:
  - Investigate the five flagged workstations in the MacLab.
  - Classify every reported message as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
  - Check the official records on the instructor's computer at the front.
  messages:
  - id: l5_account_verification
    channel: 1
    sender: UPHSL IT Services
    senderDetail: it-services@uphsl-binan-accounts.example
    subject: 'Final notice: your campus account will be suspended'
    body: Our system shows your UPHSL Campus Account failed the annual security verification.
      Your email, LMS and campus Wi-Fi access will be suspended in 12 hours. Verify
      your username and password now to keep your access.
    link: uphsl-binan-accounts.example/verify
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The address uses uphsl-binan-accounts.example, not the university's
      own uphsl-binan.example. There was no earlier notice, a 12-hour suspension
      threat adds pressure, and it asks for your password through its own link. UPHSL
      IT never asks for passwords by email - check your account by opening the student
      portal yourself.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: MAC-31
    requestedAction: Enter your username and password on a linked page within 12
      hours.
    linkNote: uphsl-binan-accounts.example/verify (training placeholder, never opened).
    clues:
    - Claims to be UPHSL IT Services.
    - 'Deadline: 12 hours.'
    - Compare the domain with Official Contacts on the instructor's computer.
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Display name
      finding: It says 'UPHSL IT Services' - but anyone can type any display name.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: displayname
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: uphsl-binan-accounts.example is not the university's domain. Official
        mail comes only from uphsl-binan.example (Official Contacts on the instructor's
        computer).
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: Final notice
      label: Subject
      finding: '''Final notice'' - but there was no first notice. Pressure in the
        subject line is a common trick.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: pressure
    - id: deadline
      field: 3
      match: suspended in 12 hours
      label: Deadline
      finding: A 12-hour suspension threat is there to make you act before you check.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: deadline
    - id: creds
      field: 3
      match: Verify your username and password
      label: What it asks
      finding: It wants your username and password. The Campus IT Security Notice
        says IT never asks for passwords by email.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: credentials
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: The link really goes to uphsl-binan-accounts.example/verify - a look-alike
        sign-in page, not the student portal.
      linkAddress: https://uphsl-binan-accounts.example/verify
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: Look-alike sender domain
      whyItMatters: uphsl-binan-accounts.example is not uphsl-binan.example.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: pressure
      title: '''Final notice'' with no first notice'
      whyItMatters: Invented urgency stops people from checking.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: deadline
      title: 12-hour suspension threat
      whyItMatters: Threats and short deadlines are pressure tactics.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: credentials
      title: Asks for your password
      whyItMatters: Real IT teams never ask for passwords by email.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Link to a look-alike page
      whyItMatters: Sign in only through the portal you already know.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: displayname
      title: Official-sounding name
      whyItMatters: A display name is just text - on its own it proves nothing.
      supportsVerdict: 0
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l5_lab_reminder
    channel: 0
    sender: UPHSL Campus Alerts
    senderDetail: Saved contact
    subject: 
    body: 'UPHSL Alerts: MacLab 3 is reserved today 1:00-4:00 PM for the CCS cybersecurity
      workshop. Bring your student ID. No reply needed.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: Fictional training example. The workshop is on this week's campus
      schedule.
    explanation: It comes from the saved UPHSL Campus Alerts contact, matches the
      workshop listed in This Week on Campus, has no link and asks for nothing. This
      is a fictional example - an unexpected request for a code, a password or a
      payment would still need checking.
    trigger: 1
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Nothing - it's a reminder.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - From the saved contact UPHSL Campus Alerts.
    - No link, no reply needed.
    - Compare with This Week on Campus.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l5_scholarship
    channel: 1
    sender: UPHSL Scholarship & Grants Office
    senderDetail: grants@uphsl-scholarship-awards.example
    subject: Congratulations! You've been pre-selected for a $2,000 grant
    body: You have been pre-selected for the 2026 Excellence Grant. To release your
      funds, complete the claim form with your student number, date of birth, bank
      account details and a photo of your ID within 48 hours. A $15 processing fee
      applies.
    link: uphsl-scholarship-awards.example/claim
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: You never applied for a grant, the address is uphsl-scholarship-awards.example
      instead of the university's domain, it asks for your bank details and an ID
      photo on an unverified site, gives a 48-hour deadline and charges a fee to
      receive money. The real Scholarships office uses the student portal and never
      charges fees.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: MAC-15
    requestedAction: Send student number, date of birth, bank details and an ID photo,
      and pay a $15 fee, within 48 hours.
    linkNote: uphsl-scholarship-awards.example/claim (training placeholder, never
      opened).
    clues:
    - A grant you never applied for.
    - Fee plus sensitive personal details.
    - Compare with Scholarships & Payments.
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Display name
      finding: '''UPHSL Scholarship & Grants Office'' sounds official - the name
        alone proves nothing.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: uphsl-scholarship-awards.example isn't the university. The real office
        is scholarships@uphsl-binan.example.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
    - id: offer
      field: 2
      match: pre-selected
      label: The offer
      finding: '''Pre-selected'' for a grant you never applied for (your case notes).'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: notapplied
    - id: data
      field: 3
      match: student number, date of birth, bank account details and a photo of your
        ID
      label: What it asks
      finding: That's enough to steal your identity and your money. Real scholarship
        offices never ask for this by email.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: data
    - id: deadline
      field: 3
      match: within 48 hours
      label: Deadline
      finding: A short deadline leaves no time to check.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: deadline
    - id: fee
      field: 3
      match: A $15 processing fee applies
      label: Fee
      finding: A genuine grant never costs money to receive.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: fee
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: uphsl-scholarship-awards.example/claim is an unverified website, not
        the student portal.
      linkAddress: https://uphsl-scholarship-awards.example/claim
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: notapplied
      title: A grant you never applied for
      whyItMatters: Unexpected money is the bait.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: domain
      title: Unofficial domain
      whyItMatters: uphsl-scholarship-awards.example is not uphsl-binan.example.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: data
      title: Asks for ID photo and bank details
      whyItMatters: Identity and banking details are what scammers want.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: fee
      title: Fee to receive money
      whyItMatters: Real scholarships never charge to pay you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: deadline
      title: 48-hour deadline
      whyItMatters: Pressure to act before verifying.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Unverified claim website
      whyItMatters: Apply and claim only through the student portal.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Official-sounding office name
      whyItMatters: Names are easy to copy - check the address.
      supportsVerdict: 0
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l5_campus_announcement
    channel: 1
    sender: UPHSL Office of Student Affairs
    senderDetail: studentaffairs@uphsl-binan.example
    subject: Campus Wi-Fi maintenance this Saturday
    body: The campus Wi-Fi will be unavailable on Saturday from 6:00 to 9:00 AM for
      scheduled maintenance. No action is needed from you. The schedule is also posted
      on the student portal and on the display in the MacLab.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: Fictional training example. The sender domain and the schedule
      match the university's official records.
    explanation: It comes from studentaffairs@uphsl-binan.example - the university's
      official domain - it describes an ordinary maintenance window that matches
      This Week on Campus and the notice on the MacLab display, and it asks for nothing.
      A fictional example, not a promise that every email in this style is safe.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: MAC-20
    requestedAction: Nothing - plan around the outage.
    linkNote: No link. It points you to the portal and the MacLab display.
    clues:
    - 'Specific time: Saturday 6:00-9:00 AM.'
    - Asks for nothing.
    - Compare with the MacLab display and This Week on Campus.
    inspectables:
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: studentaffairs@uphsl-binan.example uses the university's only official
        domain (Official Contacts).
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
    - id: when
      field: 3
      match: Saturday from 6:00 to 9:00 AM
      label: Details
      finding: A specific, ordinary maintenance window - nothing to do but plan around
        it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: specific
    - id: noaction
      field: 3
      match: No action is needed from you
      label: What it asks
      finding: 'It asks for nothing: no link, no password, no payment.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: noask
    - id: posted
      field: 3
      match: on the display in the MacLab
      label: Where to check
      finding: You can confirm it yourself - the same notice is on the display at
        the front of the lab.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: 
      label: Subject
      finding: Plain and calm. It doesn't prove anything on its own, but there's
        no pressure.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: Official university domain
      whyItMatters: uphsl-binan.example is listed in Official Contacts.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: specific
      title: Specific, ordinary details
      whyItMatters: A real maintenance notice names the date and time.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: noask
      title: Asks for nothing risky
      whyItMatters: No link, password, code or payment is requested.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: matches
      title: Matches the lab display and records
      whyItMatters: You can verify it independently.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l5_malicious_attachment
    channel: 1
    sender: Office of the Registrar
    senderDetail: registrar@upshl-binan.example
    subject: Revised enrolment requirements - open the attachment today
    body: 'Please review the attached revised enrolment requirements before your
      classes are affected. When the file opens, click Enable Content so the form
      displays correctly.

      Attachment: Enrolment_Requirements_2026.pdf.exe
      (48 KB)'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The address is upshl-binan.example - two letters swapped compared
      with the real uphsl-binan.example. The 'PDF' actually ends in .exe, so it's
      a program, and 'Enable Content' would let hidden code run. The Registrar posts
      requirements on the student portal and never sends them as attachments.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: MAC-11
    requestedAction: Open an attached file and click Enable Content.
    linkNote: No link - the danger is the attachment (simulated, never opened).
    clues:
    - Unexpected attachment from 'the Registrar'.
    - Asks you to Enable Content.
    - Compare the domain spelling with Official Contacts.
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Display name
      finding: '''Office of the Registrar'' is a real office''s name - copying it
        is easy.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: 'Look closely: upshl-binan.example - ''upshl'' instead of ''uphsl''.
        The real domain is uphsl-binan.example.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: lookalike
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: open the attachment today
      label: Subject
      finding: An unexpected attachment plus 'today'. Requirements are posted on
        the student portal (This Week on Campus).
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgent
    - id: enable
      field: 3
      match: click Enable Content
      label: Instruction
      finding: '''Enable Content'' switches on macros - a common way documents run
        hidden code.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: macros
    - id: file
      field: 3
      match: Enrolment_Requirements_2026.pdf.exe
      label: Attachment
      finding: 'It ends in .exe: a program pretending to be a PDF. Opening it could
        install malware. (Simulated - nothing is opened.)'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: doubleext
    evidence:
    - id: lookalike
      title: Misspelled university domain
      whyItMatters: upshl-binan.example imitates uphsl-binan.example.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: doubleext
      title: '''.pdf.exe'' attachment'
      whyItMatters: A double extension hides a program inside a fake document name.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: macros
      title: Asks you to Enable Content
      whyItMatters: Macros in unexpected files can run malicious code.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: urgent
      title: Unexpected attachment with urgency
      whyItMatters: Real offices post requirements on the portal.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Real office name
      whyItMatters: A copied name proves nothing.
      supportsVerdict: 0
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l5_impersonation
    channel: 1
    sender: UPHSL Accounting Office
    senderDetail: uphsl.accountingoffice@quickmail.example
    subject: 'URGENT: tuition balance - pay today to avoid being dropped'
    body: Due to an internal audit, tuition payments must now be sent to our new
      bank account (Account 0042-7781-9930). Settle your remaining balance today
      or you will be dropped from your classes. Do not call the office - our lines
      are busy. Reply with your payment receipt.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: A university office wouldn't use a free quickmail.example address,
      your tuition is already paid, payment details never change by email, and 'don't
      call the office' stops you from checking with the real Accounting Office on
      +1 (555) 010-2200. Threats plus changed bank details are classic impersonation.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: MAC-06
    requestedAction: Send a tuition payment to a new bank account today.
    linkNote: No link - it wants a bank transfer.
    clues:
    - Claims to be the Accounting Office.
    - New bank account and a same-day threat.
    - Compare with Scholarships & Payments and your case notes.
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Display name
      finding: '''UPHSL Accounting Office'' - a name anyone can type.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: quickmail.example is a free email service. University offices use
        uphsl-binan.example.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: freemail
    - id: bank
      field: 3
      match: new bank account
      label: Payment change
      finding: A sudden change of bank details is a classic payment-redirection scam.
        Payment details never change by email.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: newbank
    - id: balance
      field: 3
      match: Settle your remaining balance today
      label: The claim
      finding: Your case notes say your tuition is already fully paid.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: paid
    - id: threat
      field: 3
      match: you will be dropped from your classes
      label: Threat
      finding: A threat to drop you pushes you to pay before checking.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: threat
    - id: nocall
      field: 3
      match: Do not call the office
      label: No calls
      finding: Stopping you from calling is how impostors avoid being caught. Call
        the number in Official Contacts.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: nocall
    evidence:
    - id: freemail
      title: Free email address
      whyItMatters: Offices use the university domain, not free mail.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: newbank
      title: Changed bank details
      whyItMatters: Payment details never change by email.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: paid
      title: Your tuition is already paid
      whyItMatters: The claim contradicts your own records.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: threat
      title: Threat to drop you from classes
      whyItMatters: Fear is used to rush payment.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: nocall
      title: '''Do not call the office'''
      whyItMatters: Impostors block the one check that would expose them.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Office name in the sender
      whyItMatters: Copied names prove nothing.
      supportsVerdict: 0
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l5_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: MacLab Coordinator
    senderDetail: labs@uphsl-binan.example
    subject: Welcome to MacLab 3
    body: Welcome to MacLab 3. Please log out when you finish and report anything
      suspicious to security@uphsl-binan.example. Official notices are always posted
      on the student portal.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  caseFiles:
  - title: Campus IT Security Notice
    body: "<b>UPHSL Bi\xF1an IT Services</b>\n\n\u2022  We will <b>never</b> ask
      for your password, verification codes or personal details by email, text or
      chat.\n\u2022  Account checks happen only when <b>you</b> sign in to the student
      portal (bookmark: portal.uphsl-binan.example).\n\u2022  Never open unexpected
      attachments, and never click <b>Enable Content</b> on a document you didn't
      expect.\n\u2022  Report suspicious messages to security@uphsl-binan.example."
  - title: Official Contacts & Domains
    body: "The university's only email domain is <b>uphsl-binan.example</b>.\n\n\u2022 
      IT Services - it@uphsl-binan.example\n\u2022  Student Affairs - studentaffairs@uphsl-binan.example\n\u2022 
      Registrar - registrar@uphsl-binan.example\n\u2022  Scholarships - scholarships@uphsl-binan.example\n\u2022 
      Accounting - accounting@uphsl-binan.example \xB7 +1 (555) 010-2200\n\u2022 
      Campus Alerts - texts from the saved contact \"UPHSL Campus Alerts\"\n\nNot
      official: uphsl-binan-accounts, uphsl-scholarship-awards, upshl-binan (note
      the spelling), and any free email service."
  - title: Scholarships & Payments
    body: '<b>Scholarships:</b> students apply through the student portal. The office
      never charges a fee and never asks for bank details or ID photos by email.


      <b>Tuition:</b>
      paid only through the student portal or at the campus cashier. Payment details
      never change by email - if in doubt, call Accounting on +1 (555) 010-2200.'
  - title: This Week on Campus
    body: "\u2022  <b>Today 1:00-4:00 PM</b> - MacLab 3 reserved for the CCS cybersecurity
      workshop.\n\u2022  <b>Saturday 6:00-9:00 AM</b> - scheduled campus Wi-Fi maintenance
      (no action needed).\n\u2022  Enrolment requirements for next term are posted
      on the student portal - not sent as attachments."
  firstMessageDelay: 8
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 8, y: 14}
  minimumActivityDelay: 4
  scoring:
    basePoints: 300
    speedBonus: 30
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 60
    recoveryPoints: 70
    baseReadingSeconds: 20
    secondsPerWord: 0.5
    secondsPerDetail: 3
    minReadingSeconds: 40
    maxReadingSeconds: 120
    caseExtraSeconds: 75
    overtimeStepSeconds: 10
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 10
    maxTimePenalty: 70
    minCorrectPoints: 230
  oneStarScore: 1
  twoStarScore: 1380
  threeStarScore: 1560
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 1380
  nextLevelId: 
  nextSceneName: 
  lessons:
  - Universities send mail from one official domain - look-alike domains and free
    email accounts are red flags.
  - No real IT team asks for your password or a verification code by email or text.
  - Grants you never applied for, fees to receive money and requests for ID photos
    or bank details are scams.
  - A file ending in .exe isn't a document, and 'Enable Content' on an unexpected
    file is a trap.
  - Changed bank details, threats and 'don't call us' are signs of impersonation
    - verify with the office's known number.
  - Genuine notices match official records and ask for nothing risky.
  learningSummary: 'You investigated a campus-wide phishing wave: look-alike and
    free-mail senders, a credential trap, a fake grant, a disguised executable and
    a payment-redirection scam - and you recognised the genuine notices by checking
    them against official records.'
  performanceHigh: Outstanding! The campus network is secure - every flagged message
    was correctly identified.
  performanceModerate: Good investigation. Review the clues you missed - the official
    records separate the real notices from the fakes.
  performanceLow: Keep practising. Compare every sender, attachment and request with
    the university's official records.
